https://kc7cyber.com/challenges/1360#

Some background: Afomiya Storm is a rising social media influencer who also uses her personal email for business inquiries.
That already sets off alarm bells. If you have personal email sent to business accounts, you are more likely to open yourself up to phishing attempts. There is more information to perform reconnaissance from, which means the targeting is much more likely to work.

They have provided us with a screenshot of her Instagram and asked to find her email address. This is listed right at the top: afomiya.storm@gmail.com


Next, we are given a multiple choice question: Which of the following signs should Afomiya look for to determine if an email offering a brand deal is a phishing attempt?
A. The email contains urgent or threatening language, pressuring her to act quickly, such as "Immediate action required" or "Deadline in 24 hours."
This is true because if you act quickly, you are less likely to think critically.

B. The email asks for sensitive information like her password, credit card details, or social security number.
No one should ever need this information via email. Any legitimate reason for such information would be sent and received via more secure channels.

C. The email comes from a domain name that looks suspicious or doesn’t match the legitimate brand’s official domain.
If the domain name looks off, even slightly, it could be a spoof.

D. The email has generic greetings like "Dear Customer" instead of addressing her by name or title.
Generic greetings and statements would indicate a potential attacker mass-emailing anyone they can to see who bites.

E. All of the above.
Yes, this one is correct.


Next, it says that Afomiya is answering questions from followers. This is great for engagement, but also great for potential attackers to get some info they can use for phishing attempts or answering security questions. This is a good attempt at social engineering, which is our answer here.


And as I suspected, the attacker can use her answers to get through security questions and reset her password. They say that this was the attacker using OSINT, or Open Source Intellgence.Then, they provide a screenshot of the authentication log to identify the answers the attacker used. The question is then: What answer did the attacker enter to try and bypass the security questions? There are 2 given answers in the URL, so I entered: Kidus.


Now that the attacker has gotten through the security questions, they run into a roadblock. They can't get the code being sent to Afomiya's phone. This security measure is called two-factor authentication, which is the answer here.


Next, find Afomiya's email. This requires a query:
Employees
| where name contains "afomiya"
This shows that her email is: afomiya_storm@clouthaus.com


What is her role?
Employees
| where name contains "afomiya"
| distinct role 
Which will show us the unique role(s) associated with the name we plug in.

Now, to see if Afomiya has MFA enabled.
Employees
| where name contains "afomiya"
| distinct mfa_enabled
The answer is False.
This could allow an attack to get into her account much more easily than if 2-factor authentication was being used.


She got an email from Dior! But her alarm bells are going off. The sender's email address looks suspicious. They give us this query template:
Email
| where recipient == "enter full email"
| where subject contains "enter keyword" or links contains "enter keyword"
So plug in her email address into the second line and "Dior" into the third. This will show any emails to or from Afomiya with either subjects or links with anything to do with Dior.
The email is: collabs@dior-partners.com


Now to look for the subject line itself: [EXTERNAL] Exclusive Partnership Opportunity with Dior
As the challenge mentions, usually [EXTERNAL] is used for corporate recipients to warn users that it is not from inside the company and therefore could be suspicious. However, remember that she uses 1 address for both business and personal purposes. That makes this a little more suspicious; there is no corporate email to filter internal or external.


What is the link supplied in the email?
"https://super-brand-offer.com/login"
That link has nothing to do with Dior and looks like it will ask for credentials. This is very suspicious and asking for credentials is always malicious. There is no legitimate reason to ever need someone else's password!


Now, to confirm if she clicked the link.
OutboundNetworkEvents
| where url contains "super-brand-offer.com"
There are 2 hits. Now, I need to know what time she clicked the link: "timestamp": 2025-04-03T11:20:00.000Z


What username did she enter? 
There is this line: "url": https://super-brand-offer.com/login?username=afstorm&password=**********


Now, I have to link the domain name to an IP address.
PassiveDns
| where domain contains "enter suspicious domain"
So look for the previously found domain (minus the "https://") and there are 2 hits with the IP address of 198.51.100.12.


Next, it is mentioned that one IP can be associated with multiple domains, which would indicate a potential red flag. If one IP address registers multiple domains, they could be trying to trick people with multiple malicious URLs.
PassiveDns
| where ip contains "enter IP address"
| distinct domain
Search for the previously found IP, and there are 3 hits.


Then, to enter a few predetermined answers, including whether messages now being sent from her account are legitimate or phishing.
They are phishing attempts. The attacker is in her account right now, so they would likely be trying to get more credentials to perform lateral movement.


Reverse image search pictures she has posted of her apartment to find out where she lives - more OSINT.
She lives at The Apartments at CityCenter in Washington, D.C.


After another predetermined answer, it asks what should never be reused across accounts.
That answer is "passwords".


Our next task is to determine the IP used to gain access.
AuthenticationEvents
| where username == "afstorm"
There are 11 hits here, but with a few different IP addresses. I narrowed it down some by adding:
| where result == "Successful Login"
Just one was actually successful, from the IP of 182.45.67.89.


What domains are associated with this IP?
PassiveDns
| where ip == "182.45.67.89"
The domains are: influencer-deals.net and dior-partners.com.


What part of the User-Agent string indicates the suspicious browser and operating system? (Submit either the browser name/version or the operating system name/version.)
InboundNetworkEvents
| where src_ip == "182.45.67.89"
This finds several (27, in fact) results, but looking at the User-Agent string:
Windows NT 5.2


What country did the login originate from?
This tool can help: https://lookup.icann.org/
It will show us information for any IP we put in.
Answer: China


According to the attacker's web search history on the site, what were they trying to hack?
https://clouthaus.com/search=How+to+hack+an+influencer's+location+from+their+Instagram+story
Answer: location


According to another search log, what kind of personal info were they sneakily trying to uncover (and pretending to ask “for a friend”)?
InboundNetworkEvents
| where src_ip == "182.45.67.89"
| distinct url
| where url contains "for+a+friend"
Ansewr: https://clouthaus.com/search=Afomiya+Storm+home+address??+(asking+for+a+friend)


Which payment platform are they trying to target?
https://clouthaus.com/search=Afomiya's+Venmo+history+–+is+that+public??
Answer: Venmo


Based on another search, what shady and fake event were they pretending to plan as a way to lure Afomiya?
https://clouthaus.com/search=How+much+would+it+cost+to+hire+Afomiya+for+a+fake+birthday+party?
Answer: Fake birthday party



Next, find which external email address received messages forwarded from Afomiya’s account.
Email
| where sender == "afomiya_storm@clouthaus.com"
| where subject contains "FORWARD"
| where recipient !contains "@clouthaus"
Answer: noreply@influencer-deals.net


Which forwarded email contained Afomiya’s payment details or direct deposit form? (Submit the exact subject line.)
Email
| where sender == "afomiya_storm@clouthaus.com"
| where subject contains "FORWARD"
| where subject contains "pay" or subject contains "deposit"
There are 4 results, but only one shows: [EXTERNAL] [FORWARD] Afomiya's payment details – direct deposit form


Find which email showed a passport scan.
Email
| where sender == "afomiya_storm@clouthaus.com"
| where subject contains "FORWARD"
| where subject contains "passport"
Answer: [EXTERNAL] [FORWARD] Afomiya's passport scan – confidential


Next, look for bank statements or year-end tax documents.
Email
| where sender == "afomiya_storm@clouthaus.com"
| where subject contains "FORWARD"
| where subject contains "bank" or subject contains "tax"
There are 2. I entered: [EXTERNAL] [FORWARD] Re: Tax documents – year-end summary


Now, I just have to select what I think the attacker's goal was:
Identity theft
Financial fraud
Espionage on her brand contacts
Long-term access for future "shady business"
All of the above
I think I can safely say that the attacker's goal was all of the above, based on the entire investigation.
